      "text": "To determine the length of the tunnel, we need to analyze the situation step by step.\n\n1. **Calculate the total distance the train travels:**\n   The train is moving at a speed of 60 miles per hour. When the tail of the train exits the tunnel, it has traveled exactly 3 minutes after the front of the train entered the tunnel. Since there are 60 minutes in an hour, 3 minutes is \\(\\frac{3}{60} = \\frac{1}{20}\\) of an hour.\n   \n   Therefore, the total distance the train travels in \\(\\frac{1}{20}\\) of an hour is:\n   \\[\n   60 \\text{ miles per hour} \\times \\frac{1}{20} \\text{ hours} = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n   This distance includes the length of the train itself plus the length of the tunnel.\n\n2. **Determine the length of the train:**\n   The length of the train is given as 1 mile.\n\n3. **Set up the equation for the total distance traveled:**\n   Let the length of the tunnel be \\( L \\) miles. The total distance traveled by the train is the sum of the length of the train and the length of the tunnel. Therefore, we can write:\n   \\[\n   1 + L = 3\n   \\]\n   \n4. **Solve for \\( L \\):**\n   \\[\n   L = 3 - 1 = 2\n   \\]\n   So, the length of the tunnel is \\(\\boxed{2}\\) miles.",
