      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the length of the tunnel given the information about the train's speed and the time it takes for the tail of the train to exit the tunnel after the front of the train has entered.\n\n1. **Convert the speed of the train from miles per hour to miles per minute:**\n   The train's speed is given as 60 miles per hour. Since there are 60 minutes in an hour, we convert the speed to miles per minute:\n   \\[\n   60 \\text{ miles per hour} = \\frac{60 \\text{ miles}}{60 \\text{ minutes}} = 1 \\text{ mile per minute}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the distance traveled by the train in 3 minutes:**\n   The train travels at a speed of 1 mile per minute. In 3 minutes, the train will have traveled:\n   \\[\n   1 \\text{ mile per minute} \\times 3 \\text{ minutes} = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n   This distance of 3 miles is the length of the train, which is also the distance from the front of the train to the tail of the train when the tail exits the tunnel.\n\n3. **Determine the length of the tunnel:**\n   The tail of the train exits the tunnel exactly 3 minutes after the front of the train entered the tunnel. Since the front of the train has traveled 3 miles to reach the tail in this time, and the tunnel length plus the length of the train is the distance traveled by the front of the train, we can set up the following equation:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Length of the tunnel} + \\text{Length of the train} = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n   We know the length of the train is 1 mile, so we substitute this value into the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Length of the tunnel} + 1 \\text{ mile} = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n   Solving for the length of the tunnel, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Length of the tunnel} = 3 \\text{ miles} - 1 \\text{ mile} = 2 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the tunnel is \\(\\boxed{2}\\) miles.",
