      "text": "To determine the length of the tunnel, we need to analyze the motion of the train and how it interacts with the tunnel. Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n1. **Convert the train's speed to miles per minute:**\n   The train is moving at 60 miles per hour. Since there are 60 minutes in an hour, we convert the speed as follows:\n   \\[\n   60 \\text{ miles per hour} = \\frac{60 \\text{ miles}}{60 \\text{ minutes}} = 1 \\text{ mile per minute}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the distance the train travels while the front of the train is in the tunnel:**\n   The train exits the tunnel 3 minutes after the front of the train enters. During this 3-minute period, the train travels a distance equal to its own length plus the length of the tunnel. Let's denote the length of the tunnel by \\( L \\) miles.\n\n3. **Calculate the total distance traveled by the train in 3 minutes:**\n   Since the train travels at 1 mile per minute, in 3 minutes it travels:\n   \\[\n   1 \\text{ mile per minute} \\times 3 \\text{ minutes} = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Set up the equation for the total distance traveled:**\n   The total distance traveled by the train in 3 minutes is the sum of the length of the train and the length of the tunnel:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Length of the train} + L = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n   We know the length of the train is 1 mile, so we substitute this value into the equation:\n   \\[\n   1 \\text{ mile} + L = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Solve for the length of the tunnel:**\n   To find \\( L \\), we subtract the length of the train from both sides of the equation:\n   \\[\n   L = 3 \\text{ miles} - 1 \\text{ mile} = 2 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the tunnel is \\(\\boxed{2}\\) miles.",
