      "text": "To solve this problem, let's break it down step by step.\n\n1. **Determine the time it takes for the train to completely exit the tunnel:**\n   The tail of the train exits the tunnel 3 minutes after the front of the train entered the tunnel.\n   - Convert 3 minutes to hours: \\(3 \\text{ minutes} = \\frac{3}{60} \\text{ hours} = \\frac{1}{20} \\text{ hours}\\).\n\n2. **Calculate the distance the train travels in 3 minutes:**\n   The train is moving at a speed of 60 miles per hour. In \\(\\frac{1}{20}\\) hours, the distance traveled by the train is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} = 60 \\text{ miles per hour} \\times \\frac{1}{20} \\text{ hours} = 3 \\text{ miles}\n   \\]\n   So, the train travels 3 miles in 3 minutes.\n\n3. **Determine the length of the tunnel:**\n   When the train has traveled 3 miles (the length of the train), the front of the train exits the tunnel, and the tail of the train exits 3 minutes later. This means that the length of the tunnel is the distance the train travels in 3 minutes.\n   - The length of the tunnel is the same as the distance the train travels in 3 minutes, which is 3 miles.\n\nTherefore, the length of the tunnel is \\(\\boxed{3}\\) miles.",
